From f17b5ca300dcd68096bd9e5a0a6a2ac7e1dfd38b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oskari Rauta <oskari.rauta@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 21:57:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] clk: ingenic: keep the T41 AHB0 bus clock on

gate_ahb0 carries CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED, which only keeps the framework from
gating it in the unused-clock sweep at the end of boot. It does nothing
after a driver has taken a reference and dropped it again. U-Boot leaves
the clock running and nothing claims it, so the count sits at zero with
the hardware enabled, and the first driver to enable then disable it
switches off a bus that has been up since reset.

The ISP is that driver here - gate_csi hangs off div_ahb0 - and the SD
host is on the same bus, which the vendor tree makes explicit by placing
it at /sys/devices/platform/ahb0/13060000.msc. So once the camera has
been used and torn down, touching an inserted card is a bus transaction
that never completes: the CPU stalls with interrupts disabled, the
console dies with it, and only a power cycle recovers.

Measured on hardware across a majestic teardown, reading CPM directly
rather than trusting the framework's bookkeeping:

    streaming        CLKGR=0x3D1F57E5  CLKGR1=0x000173EE  ahb0 on
    after teardown   CLKGR=0x3F9F57E5  CLKGR1=0x000177EE  ahb0 off

The MSC's own gate stays enabled throughout; only the shared bus clock
goes. 4.4 has no CLK_IS_CRITICAL, so take a reference and never drop it,
early enough that the out-of-tree media modules cannot get there first.
---
 drivers/clk/ingenic/clk-t41.c | 38 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 38 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/clk/ingenic/clk-t41.c b/drivers/clk/ingenic/clk-t41.c
index a3a81287..0a9aaf5e 100644
--- a/drivers/clk/ingenic/clk-t41.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/ingenic/clk-t41.c
@@ -396,3 +396,41 @@ static int __init init_clk_proc(void)
 }
 
 module_init(init_clk_proc);
+
+/*
+ * Hold the AHB0 bus clock on for good.
+ *
+ * gate_ahb0 is marked CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED, which stops the framework gating it
+ * during the unused-clock sweep at the end of boot but does nothing once a
+ * driver has taken and then dropped a reference. U-Boot leaves the clock
+ * running and nothing claims it, so its reference count sits at zero with the
+ * hardware enabled - and the first driver to enable and later disable it turns
+ * off a bus that has been up since reset.
+ *
+ * On this SoC the ISP is that driver: gate_csi is parented to div_ahb0, so
+ * bringing the camera up takes the reference and tearing it down releases it.
+ * The SD host lives on the same bus (the vendor tree places it at
+ * /sys/devices/platform/ahb0/13060000.msc), so once the ISP has been used and
+ * released, any access to an inserted card is a bus transaction that never
+ * completes: the CPU stalls with interrupts off and takes the console with it,
+ * and only a power cycle recovers. Measured on hardware - CPM_CLKGR1 bit 10
+ * goes from 0 to 1 across the teardown while the MSC's own gate stays on.
+ *
+ * 4.4 has no CLK_IS_CRITICAL, so take a reference that is never dropped. This
+ * runs before any of the out-of-tree media modules are insmodded from
+ * userspace, so the count can no longer fall back to zero.
+ */
+static int __init t41_pin_bus_clocks(void)
+{
+	struct clk *ahb0 = clk_get(NULL, "gate_ahb0");
+
+	if (IS_ERR_OR_NULL(ahb0)) {
+		pr_warn("t41-clk: cannot pin gate_ahb0, SD may die with the ISP\n");
+		return 0;
+	}
+	if (clk_prepare_enable(ahb0))
+		pr_warn("t41-clk: failed to pin gate_ahb0\n");
+
+	return 0;
+}
+late_initcall(t41_pin_bus_clocks);
